{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffQwHnWKd1B2FXzWk3WE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a new model, `lempel_ziv_copy_complexity`, and it passes the self-check. But it fits much worse than the current models: its ELPD-LOO on the smoke fit was about -2959, against -2318 for the best model (`iter2_candidate3`). It will most likely be pruned at the end of the experiment.\n\n**Hypothesis.** People judge how random a sequence looks by how hard it is to describe by copying. Reading left to right, they break it into pieces, where each new piece is something already seen earlier in the sequence plus one new flip (Lempel–Ziv 1976 parsing). A sequence that breaks into more pieces looks more random. Streaks, repeated units and strict alternation are cheap to describe this way: HTHTHTHT splits into just 3 pieces, while HTTHHTHT splits into 5. People differ in how strongly this drives their choice, and each person also has a small habitual lean toward clicking left or right.\n\n**Why this one.** It responds to the two strongest findings in `critiques.md`, both of which survive the FDR correction:\n- People pick the perfectly alternating sequence less often than the best model predicts. Under this model, perfect alternation counts as non-random.\n- When two sequences switch equally often, people pick the one with more varied three-flip patterns more often than predicted. This model rewards that variety without simply counting distinct triplets, and no existing or earlier model parses sequences this way.\n\n**Model.** Each pair contributes one input: the difference in the two sequences' piece counts, scaled by log₂(n)/n, where n is the sequence length. Sensitivity varies by person and scales with a power of sequence length. The per-person left/right lean is the same component the best models use.\n\n**Check result.** The 100-draw single-chain smoke fit finished in 58 seconds. The check doesn't test 